    "['ntp clock-period [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set system ntp clock-period 17179979', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'set system ntp clock-period 17179979', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the Juniper equivalent of the Cisco `ntp clock-period` command:\\n\\n##\\nset system ntp clock-period 17179979\\n##\\n\\nNote: The `clock-period` parameter in Juniper is not a commonly used configuration item (it's typically an internal calibration value), and this exact functionality might not have a direct operational equivalent in Juniper. The above translation assumes you want to preserve the exact parameter from Cisco to Juniper syntax. In most real-world scenarios, Juniper devices handle NTP clock calibration automatically.\", 'trans_template': 'set system ntp clock-period 17179979'}]]": 1,
    "['interface [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [0, 0], 'trans_command': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'unit [parameter2] disable', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'disable', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]'}]]": 2,
    "['clock priority [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set protocols ptp interface <interface-name> priority1 1', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"##\\nset protocols ptp interface <interface-name> priority1 1\\n##\\n\\nNote: In Juniper, the equivalent PTP (Precision Time Protocol) configuration would use `priority1` (which corresponds to the clock quality priority) instead of Cisco's `clock priority` command. Replace `<interface-name>` with the actual interface name (e.g., `ge-1/0/0` or `ge-2/0/0`). \\n\\nIf the context is not PTP but another clock synchronization protocol, the command may differ. The translation assumes PTP since Cisco's `clock priority` is commonly used in PTP configurations.\", 'trans_template': 'set protocols ptp interface <interface-name> priority1 1'}]]": 1,
    "['ntp broadcast', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set protocols ntp broadcast interface ge-1/0/0', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the Juniper equivalent of the Cisco `ntp broadcast` interface command:\\n\\n##\\nset protocols ntp broadcast interface ge-1/0/0\\n##\\n\\nNote: For GigabitEthernet2/0/0, you would similarly use:\\n##\\nset protocols ntp broadcast interface ge-2/0/0\\n##\\n\\nThe Juniper command enables NTP broadcast mode on the specified interface, matching the Cisco functionality. The interface naming follows Juniper\\'s convention where \"ge\" stands for GigabitEthernet.', 'trans_template': 'set protocols ntp broadcast interface ge-1/0/0'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set protocols ntp broadcast interface ge-2/0/0', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the Juniper equivalent of the Cisco `ntp broadcast` interface command:\\n\\n##\\nset protocols ntp broadcast interface ge-1/0/0\\n##\\n\\nNote: For GigabitEthernet2/0/0, you would similarly use:\\n##\\nset protocols ntp broadcast interface ge-2/0/0\\n##\\n\\nThe Juniper command enables NTP broadcast mode on the specified interface, matching the Cisco functionality. The interface naming follows Juniper\\'s convention where \"ge\" stands for GigabitEthernet.', 'trans_template': 'set protocols ntp broadcast interface ge-2/0/0'}]]": 1,
    "['clock priority [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set protocols ptp interface ge-1/0/0 priority1 1', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the Juniper equivalent of the Cisco \"clock priority 1\" command for an interface:\\n\\n##\\nset protocols ptp interface ge-1/0/0 priority1 1\\nset protocols ptp interface ge-2/0/0 priority1 1\\n##\\n\\nNote: \\n1. The command configures PTP (Precision Time Protocol) priority1 parameter, which is Juniper\\'s equivalent of Cisco\\'s clock priority for timing synchronization.\\n2. I included both interfaces (ge-1/0/0 and ge-2/0/0) to match the original Cisco configuration context, though you asked for just the command translation.', 'trans_template': 'set protocols ptp interface ge-1/0/0 priority1 1'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set protocols ptp interface ge-2/0/0 priority1 1', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the Juniper equivalent of the Cisco \"clock priority 1\" command for an interface:\\n\\n##\\nset protocols ptp interface ge-1/0/0 priority1 1\\nset protocols ptp interface ge-2/0/0 priority1 1\\n##\\n\\nNote: \\n1. The command configures PTP (Precision Time Protocol) priority1 parameter, which is Juniper\\'s equivalent of Cisco\\'s clock priority for timing synchronization.\\n2. I included both interfaces (ge-1/0/0 and ge-2/0/0) to match the original Cisco configuration context, though you asked for just the command translation.', 'trans_template': 'set protocols ptp interface ge-2/0/0 priority1 1'}]]": 1,
    "['ntp broadcast', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set protocols ntp interface ge-1/0/0 broadcast', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the Juniper equivalent of the Cisco `ntp broadcast` interface command:\\n\\n##\\nset protocols ntp interface ge-1/0/0 broadcast\\nset protocols ntp interface ge-2/0/0 broadcast\\n##\\n\\nThe translation notes:\\n1. Juniper uses `set protocols ntp interface` hierarchy instead of interface-specific commands\\n2. Interface names follow Juniper's format (ge-x/x/x instead of GigabitEthernet)\\n3. The `broadcast` keyword maintains the same functionality as Cisco's implementation\\n4. Both interfaces are configured separately as Juniper doesn't allow multiple interfaces in one command\", 'trans_template': 'set protocols ntp interface ge-1/0/0 broadcast'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set protocols ntp interface ge-2/0/0 broadcast', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the Juniper equivalent of the Cisco `ntp broadcast` interface command:\\n\\n##\\nset protocols ntp interface ge-1/0/0 broadcast\\nset protocols ntp interface ge-2/0/0 broadcast\\n##\\n\\nThe translation notes:\\n1. Juniper uses `set protocols ntp interface` hierarchy instead of interface-specific commands\\n2. Interface names follow Juniper's format (ge-x/x/x instead of GigabitEthernet)\\n3. The `broadcast` keyword maintains the same functionality as Cisco's implementation\\n4. Both interfaces are configured separately as Juniper doesn't allow multiple interfaces in one command\", 'trans_template': 'set protocols ntp interface ge-2/0/0 broadcast'}]]": 1,
